WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:08.479
A request hands a card charge to a pool, and waits up to 200 ms for the answer.

2
00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:10.420
The issuer takes 600.

3
00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:16.765
At 200 ms, get throws a TimeoutException, and the request reports a failure.

4
00:00:16.933 --> 00:00:18.668
What happens to the charge?

5
00:00:20.867 --> 00:00:22.415
Nothing stopped it.

6
00:00:22.415 --> 00:00:27.387
At about 650 ms the issuer approves, and the card is charged.

7
00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:30.231
A timeout ends your wait, not the task.

8
00:00:31.133 --> 00:00:33.586
So you call cancel(true) on its Future.

9
00:00:33.586 --> 00:00:35.221
Does that kill the thread?

10
00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:36.857
No.

11
00:00:36.857 --> 00:00:40.112
Java has no safe way to kill a thread.

12
00:00:40.267 --> 00:00:43.334
cancel(true) calls interrupt on the task's thread.

13
00:00:43.334 --> 00:00:47.384
And interrupt only sets a flag on that thread, from false to true.

14
00:00:48.867 --> 00:00:51.234
Three tasks, on three pool threads.

15
00:00:51.234 --> 00:00:53.467
The sleeper sleeps for 5 seconds.

16
00:00:53.733 --> 00:00:59.137
The checker loops over slices of CPU work, and checks the flag before each slice.

17
00:00:59.467 --> 00:01:03.036
The ignorer runs the same loop, and never looks at the flag.

18
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:07.471
At 100 ms, main calls cancel(true) on all three.

19
00:01:07.471 --> 00:01:08.851
Which ones stop?

20
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:12.673
All three flags go up.

21
00:01:12.673 --> 00:01:14.492
The sleeper stops at once.

22
00:01:14.492 --> 00:01:17.011
The checker stops at its next check.

23
00:01:17.011 --> 00:01:18.691
The ignorer keeps going.

24
00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:19.343
Why?

25
00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:23.287
The sleeper was blocked in sleep, which watches the flag.

26
00:01:23.287 --> 00:01:27.577
It wakes up, clears the flag, and throws InterruptedException.

27
00:01:27.867 --> 00:01:31.692
The checker looked at the flag before its next slice, and returned.

28
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.776
The ignorer never looks.

29
00:01:33.776 --> 00:01:38.733
Nothing outside can stop a running loop, so it runs all 400 slices.

30
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:40.614
And the futures?

31
00:01:40.614 --> 00:01:45.304
All three said cancelled the moment cancel was called, even the ignorer's.

32
00:01:45.304 --> 00:01:48.473
If its work posted to a ledger, the post happened.

33
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:54.633
So a long CPU loop should check the flag itself, in Java or in Kotlin.

34
00:01:54.633 --> 00:01:57.293
Blocking calls like sleep already do.

35
00:01:58.067 --> 00:01:58.680
The rule.

36
00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:01.270
cancel(true) is a request, not a kill.

37
00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:06.449
A task stops only if it blocks in an interruptible call, or checks the flag.

38
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.615
Check yourself.

39
00:02:07.615 --> 00:02:12.827
A task runs a CPU loop that never checks the flag, and you call cancel(true).

40
00:02:12.827 --> 00:02:16.753
What does isCancelled return, and when does the task stop?

41
00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:20.346
True, at once.

42
00:02:20.346 --> 00:02:23.253
And the task stops only when its loop ends.
